<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as amirs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amir, meaning commander or prince in Arabic, is a title used across the Islamic world for military commanders, governors and rulers. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> amirs led armies and provinces, while in later states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> the title denoted powerful leaders and nobles. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> the rulers of the constituent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> held the title of emir.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amir.jpg" alt="Amir" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuja_Shah_Durrani_and_Men_in_his_decorated_palace_Wellcome_V0050531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
